  "worked_solution": "We're given that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=-{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x).\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(x)={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)-{e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)=2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /4}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
